        P("Immediate (IgE, minutes to 1 hour): peanut allergy, oral allergy (food pollen) syndrome. Delayed (hours, vomiting or diarrhea): FPIES, allergic proctocolitis. Food allergy affects 5% of children and 3 to 4% of adults, but 3 to 35% report one, and over 20% change their diet because of it."),
        flow(("Skin barrier defect", "Atopic dermatitis lets food protein in through the skin"), ("Sensitization", "Immune system learns to make IgE to the food"), ("Allergy", ""), caption="Dual antigen hypothesis: sensitization via skin, tolerance via the gut"),
        callout("key", P("<strong>Introduce allergenic foods early</strong> (4 to 6 months) and do not rub food on the skin. The LEAP trial showed early peanut protects high-risk infants (severe eczema or egg allergy). Maternal diet restriction in pregnancy or breastfeeding has no evidence.")),
        P("Diagnosis starts with the history: which food, a top allergen, reaction on repeat exposure, onset within minutes, and symptoms (skin most common, then breathing, cardiac, GI). Skin or IgE tests confirm or refute. The <strong>oral food challenge is the gold standard</strong>, used when history and tests disagree."),
